.TH BUCKLE\-LIBINPUT.SERVICE "8" "September 2026" "Nostalgia bucklespring keyboard sound" "System Manager Configuration"
.SH NAME
buckle\-libinput.service \- systemd user unit for the bucklespring keyboard sound
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B buckle\-libinput.service
.PP
.B /usr/lib/systemd/user/buckle\-libinput.service
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.BR systemd (1)
user unit which runs
.BR buckle\-libinput (1)
for the duration of a desktop session, so that the keyboard sounds like an IBM
Model M without anything having to be started by hand.
.PP
The unit is shipped disabled: a program that makes a noise on every keystroke
is better asked for than sprung on you.  Enable it, for your own account only,
with
.PP
.RS
.B systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now buckle\-libinput
.RE
.PP
and undo that with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user disable \-\-now buckle\-libinput" .
Neither needs root.
.SH SESSION LIFETIME
The unit is wanted by
.IR graphical\-session.target ,
and declared
.I PartOf
it, so it follows that target, which the desktop session starts at login and
which is stopped again at logout.
So the sound follows the session on the local seat: it begins when you log in
there and ends when you log out.
.PP
That is deliberately not
.IR default.target .
Where the account has lingering enabled, see
.BR loginctl (1),
the per user service manager is started at boot and kept running until
shutdown, whether or not anybody is logged in; a unit wanted by
.I default.target
would then play to an empty room.
.PP
There is no equivalent for a login on a plain text console.  systemd has a
target for the graphical session but none for a session on a tty, and
.B systemctl \-\-user
units live in one service manager per user rather than one per session, so
there is nothing for such a unit to follow.  On the console start it by hand.
.SH CONDITIONS
The unit carries a
.I ConditionPathExists
on
.IR /etc/bucklespring/uaccess ,
the flag file which arms the udev rule that grants the user at the local seat
read access to the input devices.  Until that access has been granted
.BR buckle\-libinput (1)
has nothing to listen to, so the unit stays inert rather than starting a
process which can only fail.  The comments in the udev rule,
.IR 70\-bucklespring\-libinput.rules ,
say how to arm it and what it costs you.
.PP
When granting the access some other way, such as through the
.I input
group, override the condition with
.PP
.RS
.B systemctl \-\-user edit buckle\-libinput
.RE
.PP
putting
.I ConditionPathExists=
on a line of its own in the drop-in to clear it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/buckle\-libinput.service
The unit itself.  Do not edit it; it is replaced on upgrade.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/buckle\-libinput.service.d/
Drop-in directory for local changes, as created by
.BR "systemctl \-\-user edit buckle\-libinput" .
.TP
.I /etc/bucklespring/uaccess
Flag file the unit is conditioned on.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR buckle\-libinput (1),
.BR buckle\-x11.service (8),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR loginctl (1)
